    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-332104",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14226763/assets/82d01729627b5871739120d2e512cf54610eaaf43f6f2f2c454b7e39803937a2.png)\n\nConsider a function $f(t)$ for which the graph of the Laplace transform $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-2t}f(t)\\}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-2t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $2$ units to the left.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14226763/assets/a902bac22ba86af8de8b49d5aba5bb34f35433230f303432e1f4327708f41a7d.png)",
